WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:07.200
"The Negative Effects of
Cold Plunges for Building Muscle"

00:00:07.200 --> 00:00:08.240
In my last video,

00:00:08.240 --> 00:00:10.400
I talked about how
the benefits of cold plunges

00:00:10.400 --> 00:00:13.120
appear to be due
to the placebo effect.

00:00:13.120 --> 00:00:15.760
But cold-water immersion
isn’t just futile

00:00:15.760 --> 00:00:19.760
but may be a foe when it comes
to our muscle mass.

00:00:19.760 --> 00:00:22.880
A key take-home message is
that if the goal of training

00:00:22.880 --> 00:00:24.880
is to build muscle mass
and strength,

00:00:24.880 --> 00:00:29.360
regular cold plunges after
exercise may not be beneficial.

00:00:29.360 --> 00:00:31.840
It all started
with this study in 2006,

00:00:31.840 --> 00:00:34.080
showing that cold-water
immersion of muscles

00:00:34.080 --> 00:00:37.040
may actually interfere
with regenerative processes

00:00:37.040 --> 00:00:40.640
and retard rather than support
the desired improvements

00:00:40.640 --> 00:00:42.720
of muscular performance.

00:00:42.720 --> 00:00:45.200
Since then, independent
corroborating evidence

00:00:45.200 --> 00:00:48.640
has continued to accumulate
that post-exercise cooling

00:00:48.640 --> 00:00:51.680
diminishes muscle adaptations
to strength training,

00:00:51.680 --> 00:00:55.360
leading to punny
click-bait titles like

00:00:55.360 --> 00:00:58.880
"Cryotherapy: Are we freezing
the benefits of exercise?",

00:00:58.880 --> 00:01:01.440
"Is the ice bath
finally melting?",

00:01:01.440 --> 00:01:06.240
"The cold truth," "Cooling down
the use of cryotherapy."

00:01:06.240 --> 00:01:08.800
Here’s a typical study showing
that cold plunges

00:01:08.800 --> 00:01:11.120
substantially attenuate
long-term gains

00:01:11.120 --> 00:01:13.440
in muscle mass and strength.

00:01:13.440 --> 00:01:14.720
Twelve weeks
of strength training

00:01:14.720 --> 00:01:16.320
with just typical
active recovery

00:01:16.320 --> 00:01:19.760
led to a significant increase
in muscle mass in the quads,

00:01:19.760 --> 00:01:22.000
but the same amount of training
with cold plunges

00:01:22.000 --> 00:01:23.840
barely moved the needle.

00:01:23.840 --> 00:01:27.200
And this manifested in
significantly less strength,

00:01:27.200 --> 00:01:29.920
whether we’re talking
maximum leg-press strength,

00:01:29.920 --> 00:01:31.760
knee extension,
isometric torque,

00:01:31.760 --> 00:01:34.160
or rate of force development.

00:01:34.160 --> 00:01:36.960
Note that smaller muscles
don’t necessarily lead

00:01:36.960 --> 00:01:38.080
to less strength.

00:01:38.080 --> 00:01:40.480
In this study,
the blunted muscle fiber growth

00:01:40.480 --> 00:01:43.840
didn’t translate to
impaired strength development,

00:01:43.840 --> 00:01:46.480
but post-exercise cold plunges
should be avoided

00:01:46.480 --> 00:01:49.440
if you do actually want
bigger muscles.

00:01:49.440 --> 00:01:51.520
But there are folks,
like long-distance runners,

00:01:51.520 --> 00:01:53.520
who may not want
that extra bulk;

00:01:53.520 --> 00:01:57.600
so, cold plunges may be a way
to keep their muscles smaller.

00:01:57.600 --> 00:01:59.280
This is probably
the most rigorous study

00:01:59.280 --> 00:02:00.880
involving measuring muscle mass

00:02:00.880 --> 00:02:03.840
using the gold standard,
MRI imaging,

00:02:03.840 --> 00:02:06.800
and actually taking muscle
biopsies before and after.

00:02:06.800 --> 00:02:09.200
And, while both groups gained
muscle mass and strength

00:02:09.200 --> 00:02:10.000
following training,

00:02:10.000 --> 00:02:12.640
these gains were
significantly smaller

00:02:12.640 --> 00:02:14.400
in the cold-water
immersion group,

00:02:14.400 --> 00:02:16.320
compared with
the active recovery group.

00:02:16.320 --> 00:02:19.280
And the cross-sectional area
of fast-twitch muscle fibers

00:02:19.280 --> 00:02:22.240
also increased
only in the control group.

00:02:22.240 --> 00:02:23.120
There have been all sorts

00:02:23.120 --> 00:02:24.880
of published points
and counterpoints

00:02:24.880 --> 00:02:28.960
about the cold-water dampening
effect on hypertrophy gains.

00:02:28.960 --> 00:02:31.360
Here’s a systematic review
with meta-analysis

00:02:31.360 --> 00:02:33.200
of all such studies.

00:02:33.200 --> 00:02:37.440
A harmful effect of cold-water
immersion was verified

00:02:37.440 --> 00:02:40.400
for every single
strength parameter measure:

00:02:40.400 --> 00:02:44.000
one-rep maximum,
maximal isometric strength,

00:02:44.000 --> 00:02:46.080
strength endurance,
and ballistic efforts

00:02:46.080 --> 00:02:49.200
all reduced by cold plunges.

00:02:49.200 --> 00:02:52.080
So, it appears useless
for endurance training

00:02:52.080 --> 00:02:54.880
and deleterious
for strength training.

00:02:54.880 --> 00:02:56.480
Why are people still doing it?

00:02:56.480 --> 00:03:00.800
Here’s a 2024 meta-analysis,
and the title says it all:

00:03:00.800 --> 00:03:04.320
Throwing cold water
on muscle growth.

00:03:04.320 --> 00:03:06.480
This may be because
cold-water immersion

00:03:06.480 --> 00:03:10.320
puts the chill
on muscle protein synthesis,

00:03:10.320 --> 00:03:12.640
both in the hours immediately
following exercise

00:03:12.640 --> 00:03:15.360
and during weeks
of strength training.

00:03:15.360 --> 00:03:16.320
Another explanation

00:03:16.320 --> 00:03:18.160
for cold-induced
reductions in strength

00:03:18.160 --> 00:03:20.800
is that cold-water immersion
constricts the arteries

00:03:20.800 --> 00:03:23.040
and causes a reduction
in blood flow

00:03:23.040 --> 00:03:25.840
which is important for muscle
strength and development.

00:03:25.840 --> 00:03:28.720
A 50 percent drop
in femoral blood flow,

00:03:28.720 --> 00:03:31.200
compared to warmer water
or control.

00:03:31.200 --> 00:03:34.480
Reductions in muscle blood flow
reduces the oxygen supply,

00:03:34.480 --> 00:03:37.600
which plays a role in
the decreased muscle mass, too.

00:03:37.600 --> 00:03:42.240
Cold plunges also blunt
the testosterone response.

00:03:42.240 --> 00:03:45.360
So, the impaired testosterone
response may be another reason

00:03:45.360 --> 00:03:48.480
for the attenuated strength
and muscle growth.

00:03:48.480 --> 00:03:51.840
So, cold plunges following
resistance exercise sessions

00:03:51.840 --> 00:03:54.240
should be discouraged,

00:03:54.240 --> 00:03:57.520
but they are said not to impair
aerobic training.

00:03:57.520 --> 00:03:59.120
That’s not always the case,
though,

00:03:59.120 --> 00:04:03.120
as some studies show a reduction
in aerobic fitness as well.

00:04:03.120 --> 00:04:07.040
This study found that muscle
warming was better than cooling,

00:04:07.040 --> 00:04:09.920
thought due to the slowing
of glycogen resynthesis

00:04:09.920 --> 00:04:11.280
in the cold.

00:04:11.280 --> 00:04:14.640
Mean power output was
better preserved hours after

00:04:14.640 --> 00:04:17.040
an all-out arm-cycling exercise.

00:04:17.040 --> 00:04:19.600
The cold did worse than control,

00:04:19.600 --> 00:04:21.040
and after the muscle heating,

00:04:21.040 --> 00:04:25.360
there was hardly any decrease
in power output at all.

00:04:25.360 --> 00:04:28.560
So, wait, should we instead
be taking a warm bath or sauna

00:04:28.560 --> 00:04:29.280
after exercise

00:04:29.280 --> 00:04:31.360
instead of an ice-water bath?

00:04:31.360 --> 00:04:33.600
Regardless,
cryotherapy is apparently

00:04:33.600 --> 00:04:37.884
not as cool as it seems.

